jb — a git-inspired agentic coding loop
========================================

jb is a minimal agentic coding loop: a single C binary that reads a
prompt from stdin, runs the agent loop against any OpenAI-compatible
Chat Completions endpoint (cloud or local), and streams the model's
answer to stdout.  Zero runtime dependencies except curl(1) and POSIX.

The workflow borrows git's shape:

    jb init                          # create .jb/ in the project
    echo "hello" | jb run            # run a session (real API call)
    jb commit 9f3c2a1b               # finalize a completed session
    jb status                        # current session + repo summary
    jb log --graph                   # the committed forest
    jb export 9f3c2a1b /tmp/s.html   # pi viewer HTML (self-contained)

Sessions live under `.jb/sessions/<uuid>/` — `session.jsonl` (the
conversation in pi session format v3), `events.jsonl` (the live stream),
and `metadata.json` (the jb index).  jb sessions are readable and
renderable by pi's own tools, and `jb export` produces the pi viewer.

Building:

    make

Installing:

    make install          # PREFIX=/usr/local
    make install PREFIX=~ # install to ~/bin and ~/.local/share/man/man1

Configuration is git-style: global `~/.config/jb/config.json` merged
with local `.jb/config.json`, overridable per run with `-c KEY=VALUE`.
You need an API key in JB_API_KEY or OPENAI_API_KEY.  See jb(1) for the
full command reference.

